WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.800
Label the new proteins a pancreas cell makes in a few minutes.

2
00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:06.580
An hour later, where are they?

3
00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:10.620
Could the proteins themselves tell you how far they have come?

4
00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:18.990
A pulse-chase gives a brief pulse of radioactive amino acid, then a flood of the

5
00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:24.290
unlabelled one. Only the batch made during the pulse is labelled: the cohort.

6
00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:28.560
Caro and Palade did this in the guinea-pig pancreas.

7
00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:32.980
Silver grains in a photographic layer marked the label.

8
00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.700
At about 5 minutes they lay over the rough ER.

9
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:39.880
At about 20, over the Golgi.

10
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.520
At about an hour, over the storage granules.

11
00:00:44.583 --> 00:00:49.043
So the cohort's peak moves forward through a fixed order of stations, though it

12
00:00:49.043 --> 00:00:54.983
spreads out. The ER, a sorting dock, the Golgi stack, and the trans-Golgi network,

13
00:00:55.203 --> 00:00:56.203
where routes split.

14
00:00:57.017 --> 00:00:58.577
Now follow one protein.

15
00:00:59.937 --> 00:01:04.557
In the ER it gets a ready-made tree of 14 sugars, trimmed as it folds.

16
00:01:05.917 --> 00:01:10.717
In the Golgi, enzymes in successive sacs cut and rebuild it, roughly in order.

17
00:01:11.577 --> 00:01:14.617
One, mannosidase II, marks a red line.

18
00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:20.910
But these enzymes overlap from sac to sac, so the tree cannot say which sac the

19
00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:27.090
protein is in. It records the enzymes it has met: a rough odometer, not an address.

20
00:01:28.183 --> 00:01:32.963
To read the record, sample at each chase time and treat each sample with an enzyme,

21
00:01:33.343 --> 00:01:39.543
Endo H. It cuts chains still mostly mannose, but not those rebuilt past mannosidase

22
00:01:39.543 --> 00:01:44.083
II. Cut protein loses sugar and runs faster on a gel.

23
00:01:45.443 --> 00:01:48.663
Over the chase, the cut band gives way to the uncut one.

24
00:01:49.667 --> 00:01:51.367
Some proteins should stay.

25
00:01:52.927 --> 00:01:55.947
ER chaperones, which help others fold, leak forward too.

26
00:01:57.307 --> 00:01:59.247
Many end in a tag, KDEL.

27
00:02:00.487 --> 00:02:05.087
Past the ER, in slightly acidic compartments, a receptor grabs the tag.

28
00:02:06.267 --> 00:02:11.607
A coated vesicle carries both back, and in the near-neutral ER the receptor lets go.

29
00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:14.230
Now snip the tag off.

30
00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:21.330
Sean Munro and Hugh Pelham did this to the chaperone grp78, in cultured monkey

31
00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:23.990
cells. Where does it end up?

32
00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:33.540
It is secreted with the cargo, because its KDEL tag is gone.

33
00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:40.080
For soluble cargo in animal cells, forward is the default unless a protein is held,

34
00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:42.080
fetched back, or sorted out.

35
00:02:43.133 --> 00:02:47.193
For soluble cargo, the trans-Golgi network can split the routes.

36
00:02:48.493 --> 00:02:53.333
Some carriers go straight to the surface: constitutive secretion, the default door.

37
00:02:54.673 --> 00:02:59.753
In this cell, digestive enzymes are sorted into granules instead: regulated

38
00:02:59.753 --> 00:03:04.733
secretion. They wait on a shelf until a stimulus releases them.

39
00:03:05.933 --> 00:03:08.133
Does that stimulus also speed up the line?

40
00:03:09.167 --> 00:03:11.787
So where are the labelled proteins an hour later?

41
00:03:12.987 --> 00:03:14.747
Mostly in storage granules.

42
00:03:15.887 --> 00:03:20.387
A glycoprotein among them can carry a sugar record of the enzymes it met.

43
00:03:21.607 --> 00:03:24.387
For soluble cargo, going on is the default.

44
00:03:25.727 --> 00:03:27.827
Staying means being held or fetched back.

45
00:03:29.167 --> 00:03:31.847
Storing means being sorted, in ways still argued.
